Password (bandit8): VR1ljMayciFxbnUokuQmJFw6QC9VKtub

Level 8 → 9

Goal: data.txt has many lines of text, and the password is the one line that appears only once.

Command:

sort data.txt | uniq -u

How it works:

sort data.txt puts the lines in alphabetical order, so identical lines end up next to each other.
| (the pipe) takes the output of the command on its left and feeds it in as the input of the command on its right. This lets you chain small tools together.
uniq -u prints only lines that are unique (-u), meaning they have no duplicates.

Why sort has to come first: uniq doesn’t sort anything. It only compares each line with the line directly before it, so it only spots duplicates that sit side by side. Without sort, duplicates scattered through the file would be missed and wrongly treated as unique.

Related flags worth remembering:

uniq -d shows only lines that are duplicated
uniq -c shows each line with a count of how many times it appeared

Password (bandit9): EjmOSvuAu7sGAHqHVcBDPirRe9T03kxl

Level 9 → 10

Goal: data.txt is a mostly binary file. The password is one of the few human-readable strings in it, and it’s preceded by several = characters.

Command:

strings data.txt

To cut down the output (a binary file still produces a lot of short junk strings), pipe it into grep:

strings data.txt | grep '=='

How it works:

strings scans a file and prints only the runs of printable characters, skipping the binary data. By default it only shows sequences of 4 or more characters.
The grep '==' step uses the pipe from the last level: strings outputs the readable text, and grep keeps only the lines containing ==, which is where the password sits.
Running cat on a binary file can garble your terminal, so strings is the safer way to inspect one.

Password (bandit10): B0s2khmbT9u0geKuOoVGW3JZKhndE3BG

Level 10 → 11

Goal: data.txt contains the password, but it’s been encoded in base64, so it looks like gibberish.

Command:

base64 -d data.txt

How it works:

Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. It converts data into plain letters, numbers, + and / so it can travel safely through text-only systems like email. Anyone can reverse it, no key needed.
-d stands for decode, and it turns the base64 text back into the original message.
Without -d, the command would encode the file instead.

How to recognise base64: It’s a long run of letters and numbers, often ending in one or two = padding characters.It also only uses A–Z, a–z, 0–9, + and /.

Password (bandit11): pYfOY6HwUsDj5rL9UvyhU7MCmv8vN5Ro

Level 11 → 12

Goal: data.txt contains the password, but every letter has been rotated 13 places through the alphabet (ROT13).

Command:

cat data.txt | tr 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m' 'A-Za-z'

How it works:

cat data.txt prints the file, and the pipe | passes that text into tr.
tr (translate) swaps characters one-for-one. Each character in the first set is replaced by the character in the same position in the second set.
'N-ZA-Mn-za-m' is the alphabet shifted by 13, so N maps to A, O to B, and so on, wrapping round from Z to A. The second set, 'A-Za-z', is the normal alphabet.
ROT13 is its own inverse: shifting by 13 twice returns the original letter, so the same command both encodes and decodes.
Only letters are changed. Numbers and symbols pass through untouched.

Password (bandit12): GROozWPO8QyN0mGrjUkID0WCYkZiQxrN

Level 12 → 13

Goal: data.txt is a hexdump of a file that has been compressed many times over (gzip, bzip2 and tar layers mixed together). Reverse the hexdump, then unpack one layer at a time until file reports ASCII text. That text is the password.

Setup: a place to work
mktemp -d
cp ~/data.txt /tmp/tmp.AbC123
cd /tmp/tmp.AbC123
mktemp -d creates a temporary directory with a random name under /tmp and prints the path. The -d here means directory. Without it, mktemp would make an empty temporary file. You need this because your home directory is read-only, so you need somewhere you can create files.
cp source destination copies a file. Here it copies data.txt into the temp directory, so the original stays untouched.
cd moves you into that directory (covered earlier).
Step 1: Reverse the hexdump
xxd -r data.txt > data
xxd converts between binary files and hexdumps (text showing the bytes in hexadecimal).
-r means reverse: turn the hexdump back into the original binary data.
> redirects the output into a new file called data, because binary output is unreadable on screen.
Step 2: Identify the layer
file data

file (covered earlier) reads the contents of a file and reports its real type. Do this after every step, because the extension in the filename can’t be trusted. It will report one of three types in this level.

Step 3: Unpack the layer

Renaming with mv

mv data data.gz
mv old new moves or renames a file. Here it’s used purely to give the file the extension the decompression tool expects.

gzip layer

mv data data.gz
gzip -d data.gz

bzip2 layer

mv data data.bz2
bzip2 -d data.bz2
gzip and bzip2 are two different compression programs.
-d means decompress (decode). Without it, the commands would compress the file instead.
Both tools require the right extension (.gz for gzip, .bz2 for bzip2) and will refuse files that don’t match the type.
The output is the original name without the extension, so data.gz becomes data.

tar layer

tar xf data
tar works with archives, which bundle one or more files together (an archive isn’t necessarily compressed).
x means extract the contents.
f means the next argument is the file to read from.
tar doesn’t need a specific extension. It extracts a new file with a different name, which you find with ls. Run file on that new file, not the archive.
Step 4: Repeat until done

Loop:

ls to find the newest file
file <newest file>
Use the matching command from step 3
Repeat

When file says ASCII text:

cat <filename>

That prints the password for bandit13.

Summary table
file reports	Rename to	Command	Result
gzip compressed data	name.gz	gzip -d name.gz	name
bzip2 compressed data	name.bz2	bzip2 -d name.bz2	name
POSIX tar archive	no rename needed	tar xf name	new file (check ls)
ASCII text		cat name	the password
What I got wrong along the way
I renamed a gzip file to .bz2, and bzip2 rejected it. The extension has to match what file reports at that moment.
I ran file on an old archive instead of the new file that tar had just extracted, so I kept seeing the same answer.

Password (bandit13): is qQYQiHOBPR8zR61qxYqX45quvihF2uzk

Level 13 → 14

Goal: The password for bandit14 is in a file only bandit14 can read. The home directory contains a private SSH key, which lets you log in as bandit14 without a password.

Note: Connecting to localhost from inside the Bandit server is now blocked, so the key has to be copied to your own machine and used from there.

Step 1: Copy the key to your machine

Run this from your own Kali terminal, not while logged into Bandit:

scp -P 2220 bandit13@bandit.labs.overthewire.org:/home/bandit13/sshkey.private .
scp (secure copy) copies files between machines over SSH. It works like cp, but one side can be a remote machine.
-P 2220 sets the port. In scp it’s a capital P, while ssh uses a lowercase -p.
bandit13@bandit.labs.overthewire.org:/home/bandit13/sshkey.private follows the format user@host:path, which is the file to copy from.
. is the destination, meaning the current folder. Anything with a host: prefix is treated as a remote location, so a plain local path must have no colon.
Step 2: Lock down the key’s permissions
chmod 400 sshkey.private

SSH refuses to use a private key that other users on the machine could read, and shows WARNING: UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE! and ignores it. Without that key it falls back to a password prompt.

What chmod does in general

chmod (change mode) controls who is allowed to read, write or run a file. You can see a file’s permissions with ls -l:

-rw-r----- 1 kali kali 1679 sshkey.private

After the first character (the file type), the remaining nine characters are three groups of three:

Group	Who it applies to
1st three	Owner, the user who owns the file
2nd three	Group, other users in the file’s group
3rd three	Others, everyone else

Each group can have three permissions, and each has a number:

Letter	Permission	Number
r	Read	4
w	Write	2
x	Execute (run it)	1

You add up the numbers for each group to get one digit, and chmod takes three digits (owner, group, others):

400 means owner = 4 (read only), group = 0 (nothing), others = 0 (nothing)
644 means owner = 6 (read + write), group = 4 (read), others = 4 (read)
755 means owner = 7 (read + write + execute), group = 5 (read + execute), others = 5 (read + execute)

Your key started out as 0640 (rw-r-----), so the owner could read and write it and the group could read it. SSH rejected it because the group could read it. chmod 400 makes it readable by you only.

Step 3: Log in as bandit14 with the key
ssh -i sshkey.private -p 2220 bandit14@bandit.labs.overthewire.org
-i (identity file) tells SSH to authenticate with the private key file you name instead of a password.
-p 2220 is the port, in lowercase for ssh.
bandit14@... is the user you’re logging in as.
Step 4: Read the password

Once logged in as bandit14, the password is stored in a file named after the user:

cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14

Password (bandit14): aaWecNkG4FhxJQxz07uiwzVP6bJiYS65

Mistakes I made along the way
I used kali:/Desktop as the scp destination. The colon made it a remote path, so it tried to connect to a machine called kali and was refused.
I used bandit as the host instead of the full address.
I skipped chmod, so SSH ignored the key and asked for a password.

Level 14 → 15

Goal: The password for the next level is obtained by submitting the current level’s password (bandit14’s) to a service listening on port 30000 on localhost. The service checks it and replies with the next password.

Note: This level doesn’t involve reading a file. You communicate with a running program over the network.

Step 1: Log in as bandit14
ssh -i sshkey.private -p 2220 bandit14@bandit.labs.overthewire.org

This has to be done from the Bandit server itself, because the service on port 30000 only exists there. Running nc on your own Kali machine fails with “Connection refused”, since nothing is listening on port 30000 locally.

Step 2: Connect to the service
nc localhost 30000
nc (netcat) opens a raw network connection to a host and port, and lets you send and receive text over it.
localhost is the hostname that always means “this machine”. Since you’re logged into the Bandit server, localhost is the Bandit server.
30000 is the port number the service is listening on. A port is like a numbered door on a machine, with different programs listening behind different doors.

The cursor then waits with no prompt, because the service is waiting for you to send something.

Step 3: Send the password

Type the bandit14 password and press Enter:

aaWecNkG4FhxJQxz07uiwzVP6bJiYS65

The service reads one line, so the Enter key matters. It replies with the next password.

You can also send it in one command using a pipe:

echo "aaWecNkG4FhxJQxz07uiwzVP6bJiYS65" | nc localhost 30000
echo prints the text it’s given.
| (pipe) feeds that output into the command on the right, so nc sends it as if you’d typed it.
Mistakes I made along the way
Running nc on my Kali machine instead of the Bandit server. I used kali as the host, so it connected to my own computer and was refused. The service only exists on the Bandit server, so I had to log in there first and use localhost.
Using -p as the destination port. -p sets the local source port the connection comes from, not the port I’m connecting to. This is why I got “no destination” and “invalid local port kali”. The destination port is just the last argument: nc <host> <port>.
Using -r. This randomises ports, which isn’t what I needed.
Using -c. This runs a shell command after connecting, so it isn’t a way to send text to the service. Sending the password is done by typing it in, or by piping it in with echo.

Password (bandit15): pbLYuZtTg4MgaqfJx8jbA9gKKGqM68A7

Level 15 → 16

Goal: Send bandit15’s password to a service on localhost:30001 that only accepts encrypted (SSL/TLS) connections. If the password is correct, it replies with the password for bandit16.

The command
echo pbLYuZtTg4MgaqfJx8jbA9gKKGqM68A7 | openssl s_client -connect localhost:30001 -quiet
echo ... | prints the password and pipes it into the next command, so it gets sent as soon as the connection opens.
openssl s_client is a client for SSL/TLS. It performs the encrypted handshake that this service requires, which nc cannot do.
-connect localhost:30001 sets the destination as host and port in one argument, joined by a colon.
-quiet hides the certificate and session details, so only the server’s reply shows.
What the output means

The reply comes at the end, after the line Correct!, followed by the next password.

Lessons learnt
Some services need encryption before they’ll talk to you. nc sends plain text, so it’s the wrong tool for an SSL/TLS service. openssl s_client does the handshake first, then passes your input through.
Read the level goal carefully. It tells you the port and whether the service is encrypted. Port 30001 was the clue that this was a different kind of service from the one on 30000.
Pipes let you script input. echo plus | sends the password without you typing it into a live session.
Know what each flag does. -p in nc is the local port, not the destination. -quiet in openssl changes what you see, hiding the certificate and session details.
Use the same pattern on every level. Identify the service, the port, the protocol, then send the password and read the reply.

Password (bandit16): kS0Hf0u5HiXFwKMKFqXvPdOTNGGa0X8V

Level 16 → 17

Goal: Find which service on localhost in the range 31000–32000 accepts SSL/TLS connections and replies with a private SSH key when given the bandit16 password. That key logs you in as bandit17.

Step 1: Log in as bandit16
ssh -p 2220 bandit16@bandit.labs.overthewire.org

Confirm you’re on the right account with whoami.

Step 2: Scan for open ports
nmap -p 31000-32000 localhost
nmap scans a host to see which ports are open, meaning a program is listening on them.
-p 31000-32000 limits the scan to that range, so it doesn’t check all 65,535 ports.
localhost is this machine, so it scans the Bandit server.

This returns five open ports: 31046, 31518, 31691, 31790 and 31960.

Step 3: Test each open port

Send the bandit16 password to each port over an encrypted connection:

echo kS0Hf0u5HiXFwKMKFqXvPdOTNGGa0X8V | openssl s_client -connect localhost:<port> -quiet

Results:

31046, 31691: fail with an SSL error, so they aren’t speaking the encrypted protocol this level needs.
31518: completes the handshake but just echoes your password back, with no key.
31790: replies Correct! followed by the private key for bandit17. This is the one.
31960: the command has to be correct to run. A typo in openssl (s_cient) stopped it from running at all.
Step 4: Save the key on your own machine

Run this on your Kali machine, not the server. Copy the whole block from -----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY----- to -----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY----- into a file:

nano ~/Desktop/bandit17.key
chmod 400 ~/Desktop/bandit17.key

SSH refuses to use a key that others can read, so chmod 400 is required before the next step.

Step 5: Confirm the login works
ssh -i ~/Desktop/bandit17.key -p 2220 bandit17@bandit.labs.overthewire.org
Lessons learnt
Read the level goal and work out what each port should do. Only one of the open ports gave the key, so you test each one rather than assuming.
-sV made the scan very slow. A plain port scan finishes almost instantly. Add -sV only when you need service versions.
Check your prompt before running commands. The kali@kali prompt means your own machine. bandit16@bandit means the server. Files saved on the wrong one end up in the wrong place.
Watch for typos in commands. s_cient failed silently with a confusing error, so read the output before moving on.
Permissions matter for SSH keys. chmod 400 is needed before SSH will accept a private key.

Password (bandit17): Not yet retrieved. Run the login above, then cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit17 once you’re in.

Level 17 → 18

Goal: passwords.old and passwords.new sit in the home directory. Exactly one line differs between them, and the password for bandit18 is on that line.

Step 1: Compare the files
diff passwords.old passwords.new
diff compares two files line by line and shows what’s different.
The output 42c42 means line 42 differs. The < line is from the first file (passwords.old), and the > line is from the second (passwords.new).

The line marked > in passwords.new is the new password.

Lessons learnt
Don’t overthink it. diff is the right tool for “what changed between these two files”, and the output tells you the answer directly.
Read the > and < markers. > is the second file, which is where the new password lives.
Check your flags. Several of my earlier attempts used -breif, which is a typo for -brief and isn’t a valid option. The -q flag only reports whether files differ, not how, so it wouldn’t have shown the password.
